<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kebbi</h1><p class="st-meta">1516 CE – 1831 CE (approx.) · capital Birnin Kebbi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hausa kingdom founded by the rebel Songhai general Kanta, which briefly dominated Hausaland in the 16th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-kanta">Muhammadu Kanta</a>, a commander serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a>, quarrelled over the spoils of a campaign against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-agadez">Agadez</a> and rebelled. He established an independent kingdom in the Kebbi region of north-western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a> and built fortified capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surame">Surame</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-kebbi">Birnin Kebbi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-kanta">Kanta</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a> and made Kebbi the dominant power in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a>, taking tribute from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/katsina-kingdom">Katsina</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-zamfara">Zamfara</a> and others. After his death Kebbi lost much of its empire but remained a strong military state, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banza-bakwai">Banza Bakwai</a>. Its people, the Kebbawa, are farmers and fishermen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rima-river">Rima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> valleys.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1805 jihadists of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-kebbi">Birnin Kebbi</a>. The Kebbi dynasty fought on from the marshes and in 1831 established the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argungu-emirate">Argungu Emirate</a>, which resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> until British rule. The name lives on in Kebbi State.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kebbi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kebbi" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Henry B. Lovejoy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_western_Hausaland_in_the_late_18th-century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1516 CE – 1831 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Birnin Kebbi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammadu Kanta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Hausa traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kebbi, Kebbi Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
